+## Subtopics
+
+### Classical Billboards
+Single textured quad facing the camera. Cheapest, but suffers from severe parallax artifacts. Choose for axis-symmetrical objects or distant, sparse geometry [S1].
+
+### Cross Billboards
+2-4 crossing quads per object. Reduces parallax but introduces grazing angle artifacts. Choose for slightly more robust static geometry representation where performance is still critical [S1].
+
+### Particle Systems
+Dynamic billboards with lifecycle rules. Used for transient effects like smoke, fire, or dust. Choose for animated participating media approximations [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of billboards/particles. Rendering cost is low per instance, allowing millions of instances in theory, though practical limits depend on draw call overhead and texture memory [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for instance data plus texture memory. No volumetric grid storage is required [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Extremely low computational cost; suitable for real-time rendering of large-scale scenes (e.g., forests) or simple media effects [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** No parallax (classical); popping artifacts; grazing angle artifacts (cross); artist-dependent; not suitable for dynamic lighting changes or complex geometry [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Real-time rendering of forests, simple participating media effects, or when performance is the primary constraint and visual fidelity can be compromised [S1][S2, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When parallax is required, for dynamic environments with changing lighting, or for high-fidelity volume rendering [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of billboards/particles. Sorting adds $O(N \log N)$ [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing particle/billboard data plus texture memory [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Extremely fast for real-time rendering, making it the preferred choice in industrial simulators despite visual limitations [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Low computational cost, simple implementation, effective for static or pre-defined effects [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** No parallax (classical), popping artifacts, grazing angle artifacts (cross), artist-dependent, no dynamic lighting interaction [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Real-time rendering of forests, static volumetric effects (light beams), or simple particle effects where physical accuracy is not required [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Dynamic environments with changing lighting, dense media requiring accurate scattering, or when camera movement is significant and parallax is visible [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Billboards have been a common tool for real-time rendering of forests since at least the late 1990s [S1]. They are widely used in industrial simulators and video games due to their efficiency [S1][S2, unofficial]. The concept of using particles rendered as billboards for participating media was explored in the context of real-time light transport [S2, unofficial]. Cross billboards were introduced to mitigate the lack of parallax in classical billboards, though they introduce their own artifacts [S1].
